    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLeast privilege gives an identity only the permissions required for its legitimate work.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Least privilege accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Least privilege gives an identity only the permissions required for its legitimate work.\n2. Use a concrete case: Give a reporting service read access only to the tables and operations it actually needs.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Least privilege, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Least privilege first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nGive a reporting service read access only to the tables and operations it actually needs.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use Least privilege when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, give a reporting service read access only to the tables and operations it actually needs. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Least privilege as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Least privilege.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Least privilege, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
