    slug: "cyberseqcurity-what-are-the-main-trade-offs-of-least-privilege-48",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked CyberSeqcurity interview question about Least privilege.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLeast privilege is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Least privilege, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Least privilege gives you: Least privilege gives an identity only the permissions required for its legitimate work.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Least privilege, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Give a reporting service read access only to the tables and operations it actually needs.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nGive a reporting service read access only to the tables and operations it actually needs.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use Least privilege when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, give a reporting service read access only to the tables and operations it actually needs. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Least privilege as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Least privilege.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
