    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nEncryption transforms plaintext into ciphertext using a key so only authorized parties can recover the protected data.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Encryption accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Encryption transforms plaintext into ciphertext using a key so only authorized parties can recover the protected data.\n2. Use a concrete case: Encrypt sensitive data in transit or at rest when the threat model requires confidentiality.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Encryption, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Encryption first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nEncrypt sensitive data in transit or at rest when the threat model requires confidentiality.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use Encryption when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, encrypt sensitive data in transit or at rest when the threat model requires confidentiality. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Encryption as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Encryption.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Encryption, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
