    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSQL injection occurs when untrusted input changes the meaning of a database query.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define SQL injection accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: SQL injection occurs when untrusted input changes the meaning of a database query.\n2. Use a concrete case: Use a parameterized SQL statement so a user's input is treated as data rather than executable query syntax.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Faster reads can come with extra storage, write overhead, or plan complexity, so the access pattern should justify the change.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define SQL injection first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nUse a parameterized SQL statement so a user's input is treated as data rather than executable query syntax.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use SQL injection when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use a parameterized SQL statement so a user's input is treated as data rather than executable query syntax. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor SQL injection, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
