    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nXSS is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For XSS, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what XSS gives you: Cross-site scripting occurs when attacker-controlled content is executed as script in a user’s browser context.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For XSS, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Escape or safely render untrusted profile text so an attacker cannot inject executable script into another user's page.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nEscape or safely render untrusted profile text so an attacker cannot inject executable script into another user's page.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use XSS when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, escape or safely render untrusted profile text so an attacker cannot inject executable script into another user's page. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating XSS as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with XSS.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nXSS is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For XSS, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what XSS gives you: Cross-site scripting occurs when attacker-controlled content is executed as script in a user’s browser context.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For XSS, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Escape or safely render untrusted profile text so an attacker cannot inject executable script into another user's page.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nEscape or safely render untrusted profile text so an attacker cannot inject executable script into another user's page.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use XSS when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, escape or safely render untrusted profile text so an attacker cannot inject executable script into another user's page. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating XSS as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with XSS.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
