    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain XSS in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: Escape or safely render untrusted profile text so an attacker cannot inject executable script into another user's page.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Cross-site scripting occurs when attacker-controlled content is executed as script in a user’s browser context.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: Escape or safely render untrusted profile text so an attacker cannot inject executable script into another user's page.\n4. Finish with the limitation: For XSS, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nEscape or safely render untrusted profile text so an attacker cannot inject executable script into another user's page.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use XSS when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, escape or safely render untrusted profile text so an attacker cannot inject executable script into another user's page. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating XSS as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with XSS.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
