    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about XSS when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to XSS.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Cross-site scripting occurs when attacker-controlled content is executed as script in a user’s browser context.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Escape or safely render untrusted profile text so an attacker cannot inject executable script into another user's page.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For XSS, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nEscape or safely render untrusted profile text so an attacker cannot inject executable script into another user's page.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use XSS when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, escape or safely render untrusted profile text so an attacker cannot inject executable script into another user's page. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating XSS as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with XSS.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
