    slug: "cyberseqcurity-what-are-the-main-benefits-of-csrf-104",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked CyberSeqcurity interview question about CSRF.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of CSRF comes from the behavior it provides: CSRF tricks a browser into sending an authenticated request that the user did not intend to make. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Protect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: CSRF tricks a browser into sending an authenticated request that the user did not intend to make.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Protect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf.\n3. State the trade-off: For CSRF, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nProtect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use CSRF when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, protect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating CSRF as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with CSRF.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
