    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCSRF is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For CSRF, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what CSRF gives you: CSRF tricks a browser into sending an authenticated request that the user did not intend to make.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For CSRF, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Protect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nProtect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use CSRF when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, protect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating CSRF as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with CSRF.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
