    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain CSRF in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: Protect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: CSRF tricks a browser into sending an authenticated request that the user did not intend to make.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: Protect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf.\n4. Finish with the limitation: For CSRF, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nProtect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use CSRF when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, protect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating CSRF as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with CSRF.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain CSRF in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: Protect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: CSRF tricks a browser into sending an authenticated request that the user did not intend to make.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: Protect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf.\n4. Finish with the limitation: For CSRF, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nProtect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use CSRF when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, protect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating CSRF as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with CSRF.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
