    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about CSRF when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to CSRF.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: CSRF tricks a browser into sending an authenticated request that the user did not intend to make.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Protect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For CSRF, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nProtect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use CSRF when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, protect a cookie-authenticated state-changing request so another site cannot silently trigger it on the user's behalf. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating CSRF as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with CSRF.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
