    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAPI security is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Stronger controls reduce risk but can add latency, operational work, or user friction; the control should match the threat and resource being protected. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what API security gives you: API security covers authentication, authorization, validation, rate limiting, transport security, abuse resistance, and safe data exposure.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Stronger controls reduce risk but can add latency, operational work, or user friction; the control should match the threat and resource being protected.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: An API should enforce authentication, authorization, validation, rate limits, and safe error handling at its boundary.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nAn API should enforce authentication, authorization, validation, rate limits, and safe error handling at its boundary.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use API security when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an API should enforce authentication, authorization, validation, rate limits, and safe error handling at its boundary. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
