    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen SSDLC misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to SSDLC. The secure software development lifecycle integrates security activities into requirements, design, coding, testing, deployment, and operations.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls SSDLC: The secure software development lifecycle integrates security activities into requirements, design, coding, testing, deployment, and operations.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: For example, in a security-sensitive application, an engineer may run into this concept when the secure software development lifecycle integrates security activities into requirements, design, coding, testing, deployment, and operations. The implementation can then be checked against the expected behavior.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For SSDLC, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a security-sensitive application, an engineer may run into this concept when the secure software development lifecycle integrates security activities into requirements, design, coding, testing, deployment, and operations. The implementation can then be checked against the expected behavior.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use SSDLC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for example, in a security-sensitive application, an engineer may run into this concept when the secure software development lifecycle integrates security activities into requirements, design, coding, testing, deployment, and operations. The implementation can then be checked against the expected behavior. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating SSDLC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with SSDLC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
