    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Threat modeling comes from the behavior it provides: Threat modeling identifies assets, trust boundaries, attack paths, and mitigations before or during system design. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: For a file-upload feature, enumerate assets, trust boundaries, abuse cases, and controls before implementation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Threat modeling identifies assets, trust boundaries, attack paths, and mitigations before or during system design.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: For a file-upload feature, enumerate assets, trust boundaries, abuse cases, and controls before implementation.\n3. State the trade-off: For Threat modeling, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a file-upload feature, enumerate assets, trust boundaries, abuse cases, and controls before implementation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use Threat modeling when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a file-upload feature, enumerate assets, trust boundaries, abuse cases, and controls before implementation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Threat modeling as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Threat modeling.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
