    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMonitoring is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Monitoring, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Monitoring gives you: Security monitoring collects signals that help detect suspicious behavior, misuse, and control failures.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Monitoring, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: For example, in a security-sensitive application, an engineer may run into this concept when security monitoring collects signals that help detect suspicious behavior, misuse, and control failures. The implementation can then be checked against the expected behavior.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a security-sensitive application, an engineer may run into this concept when security monitoring collects signals that help detect suspicious behavior, misuse, and control failures. The implementation can then be checked against the expected behavior.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use Monitoring when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for example, in a security-sensitive application, an engineer may run into this concept when security monitoring collects signals that help detect suspicious behavior, misuse, and control failures. The implementation can then be checked against the expected behavior. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Monitoring as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Monitoring.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
