    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Kafka interview question about Consumer groups.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA consumer group coordinates consumers so that each partition is assigned to at most one active consumer in the group at a time.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Consumer groups accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: A consumer group coordinates consumers so that each partition is assigned to at most one active consumer in the group at a time.\n2. Use a concrete case: Put several consumers in one group so Kafka can assign partitions across them for parallel processing.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Consumer groups, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Consumer groups first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nPut several consumers in one group so Kafka can assign partitions across them for parallel processing.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn event-streaming platform may use Consumer groups when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, put several consumers in one group so Kafka can assign partitions across them for parallel processing. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Consumer groups as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Consumer groups.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Consumer groups, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
