    slug: "kafka-how-would-you-troubleshoot-a-problem-involving-consumer-groups-67",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Kafka interview question about Consumer groups.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Consumer groups misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Consumer groups. A consumer group coordinates consumers so that each partition is assigned to at most one active consumer in the group at a time.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Consumer groups: A consumer group coordinates consumers so that each partition is assigned to at most one active consumer in the group at a time.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Put several consumers in one group so Kafka can assign partitions across them for parallel processing.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Consumer groups, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nPut several consumers in one group so Kafka can assign partitions across them for parallel processing.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn event-streaming platform may use Consumer groups when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, put several consumers in one group so Kafka can assign partitions across them for parallel processing. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Consumer groups as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Consumer groups.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
