    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Delivery semantics misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Delivery semantics. Kafka consumers can be operated with at-least-once and other delivery behaviors depending on producer, consumer, and transaction configuration.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Delivery semantics: Kafka consumers can be operated with at-least-once and other delivery behaviors depending on producer, consumer, and transaction configuration.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Choose at-most-once, at-least-once, or an application-level exactly-once approach based on the consequence of duplicates or loss.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Delivery semantics, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nChoose at-most-once, at-least-once, or an application-level exactly-once approach based on the consequence of duplicates or loss.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn event-streaming platform may use Delivery semantics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, choose at-most-once, at-least-once, or an application-level exactly-once approach based on the consequence of duplicates or loss. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Delivery semantics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Delivery semantics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
