    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDelivery semantics is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Delivery semantics, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Delivery semantics gives you: Kafka consumers can be operated with at-least-once and other delivery behaviors depending on producer, consumer, and transaction configuration.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Delivery semantics, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Choose at-most-once, at-least-once, or an application-level exactly-once approach based on the consequence of duplicates or loss.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nChoose at-most-once, at-least-once, or an application-level exactly-once approach based on the consequence of duplicates or loss.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn event-streaming platform may use Delivery semantics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, choose at-most-once, at-least-once, or an application-level exactly-once approach based on the consequence of duplicates or loss. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Delivery semantics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Delivery semantics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
