    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Delivery semantics when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Delivery semantics.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Kafka consumers can be operated with at-least-once and other delivery behaviors depending on producer, consumer, and transaction configuration.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Choose at-most-once, at-least-once, or an application-level exactly-once approach based on the consequence of duplicates or loss.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Delivery semantics, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nChoose at-most-once, at-least-once, or an application-level exactly-once approach based on the consequence of duplicates or loss.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn event-streaming platform may use Delivery semantics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, choose at-most-once, at-least-once, or an application-level exactly-once approach based on the consequence of duplicates or loss. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Delivery semantics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Delivery semantics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
