    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Transactions misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Transactions. Kafka transactions allow a producer to atomically publish multiple records and coordinate certain consume-transform-produce workflows.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Transactions: Kafka transactions allow a producer to atomically publish multiple records and coordinate certain consume-transform-produce workflows.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: An order workflow can update related records in one database transaction when those writes must either all commit or all roll back together.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Transactions, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nAn order workflow can update related records in one database transaction when those writes must either all commit or all roll back together.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn event-streaming platform may use Transactions when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an order workflow can update related records in one database transaction when those writes must either all commit or all roll back together. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Choosing a concurrency mechanism without identifying the shared state.\n- Ignoring deadlocks, contention, or lost updates.\n- Assuming a stronger isolation level is always the best answer.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
