    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTransactions is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Transactions, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Transactions gives you: Kafka transactions allow a producer to atomically publish multiple records and coordinate certain consume-transform-produce workflows.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Transactions, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: An order workflow can update related records in one database transaction when those writes must either all commit or all roll back together.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nAn order workflow can update related records in one database transaction when those writes must either all commit or all roll back together.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn event-streaming platform may use Transactions when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an order workflow can update related records in one database transaction when those writes must either all commit or all roll back together. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Choosing a concurrency mechanism without identifying the shared state.\n- Ignoring deadlocks, contention, or lost updates.\n- Assuming a stronger isolation level is always the best answer.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
