    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Transactions when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Transactions.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Kafka transactions allow a producer to atomically publish multiple records and coordinate certain consume-transform-produce workflows.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: An order workflow can update related records in one database transaction when those writes must either all commit or all roll back together.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Transactions, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nAn order workflow can update related records in one database transaction when those writes must either all commit or all roll back together.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn event-streaming platform may use Transactions when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an order workflow can update related records in one database transaction when those writes must either all commit or all roll back together. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Choosing a concurrency mechanism without identifying the shared state.\n- Ignoring deadlocks, contention, or lost updates.\n- Assuming a stronger isolation level is always the best answer.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
