    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Kafka interview question about Retention.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nKafka retention controls how long records remain available according to time and/or size policies.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Retention accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Kafka retention controls how long records remain available according to time and/or size policies.\n2. Use a concrete case: Keep events for the replay period the business needs instead of retaining everything forever.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Retention, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Retention first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nKeep events for the replay period the business needs instead of retaining everything forever.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn event-streaming platform may use Retention when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, keep events for the replay period the business needs instead of retaining everything forever. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Retention as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Retention.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Retention, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
