    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Retention misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Retention. Kafka retention controls how long records remain available according to time and/or size policies.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Retention: Kafka retention controls how long records remain available according to time and/or size policies.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Keep events for the replay period the business needs instead of retaining everything forever.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Retention, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nKeep events for the replay period the business needs instead of retaining everything forever.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn event-streaming platform may use Retention when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, keep events for the replay period the business needs instead of retaining everything forever. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Retention as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Retention.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Retention misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Retention. Kafka retention controls how long records remain available according to time and/or size policies.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Retention: Kafka retention controls how long records remain available according to time and/or size policies.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Keep events for the replay period the business needs instead of retaining everything forever.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Retention, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nKeep events for the replay period the business needs instead of retaining everything forever.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn event-streaming platform may use Retention when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, keep events for the replay period the business needs instead of retaining everything forever. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Retention as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Retention.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    explanation: "How would you troubleshoot a problem involving Retention? is a core Kafka interview concept. A strong answer should define the concept clearly, explain how it works, show where it is useful, and discuss practical trade-offs. The candidate should connect the explanation to a realistic engineering or business scenario and distinguish the concept from closely related alternatives.",
