    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLog compaction retains the latest record for a key per partition subject to compaction rules.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Compaction accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Log compaction retains the latest record for a key per partition subject to compaction rules.\n2. Use a concrete case: Use log compaction for keyed records when retaining the latest value per key is the desired state representation.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Compaction, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Compaction first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nUse log compaction for keyed records when retaining the latest value per key is the desired state representation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn event-streaming platform may use Compaction when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use log compaction for keyed records when retaining the latest value per key is the desired state representation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Compaction as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Compaction.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Compaction, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
