    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Kafka Streams are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Kafka Streams is a library for building streaming applications that read from and write to Kafka topics.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Assuming delivery means exactly once without defining the actual semantics.\n- Ignoring consumer lag or poison messages.\n- Forgetting that partitioning and key choice affect ordering and throughput.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: Use Kafka Streams to transform or aggregate records continuously when the stream processing belongs inside the Kafka application.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nUse Kafka Streams to transform or aggregate records continuously when the stream processing belongs inside the Kafka application.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn event-streaming platform may use Kafka Streams when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use Kafka Streams to transform or aggregate records continuously when the stream processing belongs inside the Kafka application. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming delivery means exactly once without defining the actual semantics.\n- Ignoring consumer lag or poison messages.\n- Forgetting that partitioning and key choice affect ordering and throughput.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Kafka Streams. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
