    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about Requirements.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Requirements are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Requirements describe what the system must do and the constraints or quality attributes it must satisfy.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Treating Requirements as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Requirements.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: Separate a functional requirement such as creating an order from non-functional targets such as latency, availability, and security.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nSeparate a functional requirement such as creating an order from non-functional targets such as latency, availability, and security.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Requirements when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, separate a functional requirement such as creating an order from non-functional targets such as latency, availability, and security. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Requirements as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Requirements.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Requirements. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
