    slug: "systemarchitecture-what-is-scalability-21",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about Scalability.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nScalability is the ability to handle increased workload while maintaining acceptable behavior.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Scalability accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Scalability is the ability to handle increased workload while maintaining acceptable behavior.\n2. Use a concrete case: A search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Improving headroom or speed usually consumes additional compute, engineering effort, or operational complexity, so the target should be tied to observed demand.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Scalability first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Scalability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Scalability, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
