    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nScalability is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Improving headroom or speed usually consumes additional compute, engineering effort, or operational complexity, so the target should be tied to observed demand. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Scalability gives you: Scalability is the ability to handle increased workload while maintaining acceptable behavior.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Improving headroom or speed usually consumes additional compute, engineering effort, or operational complexity, so the target should be tied to observed demand.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Scalability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
