    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about Vertical scaling.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Vertical scaling when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Vertical scaling.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Vertical scaling increases the resources available to a machine or instance.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Increase the CPU or memory of one service instance when the workload still fits on a larger machine.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Vertical scaling, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nIncrease the CPU or memory of one service instance when the workload still fits on a larger machine.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Vertical scaling when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, increase the CPU or memory of one service instance when the workload still fits on a larger machine. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Vertical scaling as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Vertical scaling.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
