    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Horizontal scaling when the problem matches the behavior it provides: Horizontal scaling adds more machines or instances to spread workload. A concrete application is Add service instances behind a load balancer when the application can safely distribute requests.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Horizontal scaling provides the required behavior: Horizontal scaling adds more machines or instances to spread workload.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: Add service instances behind a load balancer when the application can safely distribute requests.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: For Horizontal scaling, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Horizontal scaling fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nAdd service instances behind a load balancer when the application can safely distribute requests.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Horizontal scaling when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, add service instances behind a load balancer when the application can safely distribute requests. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Horizontal scaling as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Horizontal scaling.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
