    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about Horizontal scaling.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Horizontal scaling misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Horizontal scaling. Horizontal scaling adds more machines or instances to spread workload.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Horizontal scaling: Horizontal scaling adds more machines or instances to spread workload.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Add service instances behind a load balancer when the application can safely distribute requests.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Horizontal scaling, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nAdd service instances behind a load balancer when the application can safely distribute requests.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Horizontal scaling when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, add service instances behind a load balancer when the application can safely distribute requests. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Horizontal scaling as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Horizontal scaling.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
