    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because load balancing distributes traffic or work across multiple targets. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: distribute requests across healthy service instances and stop routing to an unhealthy target.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Load balancing addresses it: Load balancing distributes traffic or work across multiple targets.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: Distribute requests across healthy service instances and stop routing to an unhealthy target.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: For Load balancing, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nDistribute requests across healthy service instances and stop routing to an unhealthy target.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Load balancing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, distribute requests across healthy service instances and stop routing to an unhealthy target. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Load balancing as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Load balancing.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
