    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLoad balancing is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Load balancing, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Load balancing gives you: Load balancing distributes traffic or work across multiple targets.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Load balancing, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Distribute requests across healthy service instances and stop routing to an unhealthy target.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nDistribute requests across healthy service instances and stop routing to an unhealthy target.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Load balancing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, distribute requests across healthy service instances and stop routing to an unhealthy target. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Load balancing as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Load balancing.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
