    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about Caching.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCaching stores reusable data closer to where it is needed to reduce repeated work or latency.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Caching accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Caching stores reusable data closer to where it is needed to reduce repeated work or latency.\n2. Use a concrete case: A product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: The gain in speed or load reduction has to be balanced against stale data, invalidation complexity, and memory use.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Caching first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Caching when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Caching data without an invalidation or expiry strategy.\n- Ignoring stale reads and cache stampedes.\n- Assuming a cache removes the need for a correct source of truth.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Caching, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
