    slug: "systemarchitecture-what-are-the-common-mistakes-with-caching-65",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about Caching.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Caching are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Caching stores reusable data closer to where it is needed to reduce repeated work or latency.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Caching data without an invalidation or expiry strategy.\n- Ignoring stale reads and cache stampedes.\n- Assuming a cache removes the need for a correct source of truth.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: A product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nA product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Caching when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Caching data without an invalidation or expiry strategy.\n- Ignoring stale reads and cache stampedes.\n- Assuming a cache removes the need for a correct source of truth.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Caching. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
