    slug: "systemarchitecture-what-are-the-main-trade-offs-of-caching-68",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about Caching.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCaching is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. The gain in speed or load reduction has to be balanced against stale data, invalidation complexity, and memory use. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Caching gives you: Caching stores reusable data closer to where it is needed to reduce repeated work or latency.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: The gain in speed or load reduction has to be balanced against stale data, invalidation complexity, and memory use.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Caching when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Caching data without an invalidation or expiry strategy.\n- Ignoring stale reads and cache stampedes.\n- Assuming a cache removes the need for a correct source of truth.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
