    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about Replication.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Replication are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Replication maintains multiple copies of data or services for availability, read scale, or fault tolerance.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Treating Replication as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Replication.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: Replicate a partition across brokers so a broker failure does not automatically lose the replicated data.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nReplicate a partition across brokers so a broker failure does not automatically lose the replicated data.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Replication when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, replicate a partition across brokers so a broker failure does not automatically lose the replicated data. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Replication as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Replication.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Replication. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
