    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about Replication.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Replication when the problem matches the behavior it provides: Replication maintains multiple copies of data or services for availability, read scale, or fault tolerance. A concrete application is Replicate a partition across brokers so a broker failure does not automatically lose the replicated data.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Replication provides the required behavior: Replication maintains multiple copies of data or services for availability, read scale, or fault tolerance.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: Replicate a partition across brokers so a broker failure does not automatically lose the replicated data.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: For Replication, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Replication fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nReplicate a partition across brokers so a broker failure does not automatically lose the replicated data.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Replication when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, replicate a partition across brokers so a broker failure does not automatically lose the replicated data. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Replication as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Replication.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
