    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Replication misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Replication. Replication maintains multiple copies of data or services for availability, read scale, or fault tolerance.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Replication: Replication maintains multiple copies of data or services for availability, read scale, or fault tolerance.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Replicate a partition across brokers so a broker failure does not automatically lose the replicated data.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Replication, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nReplicate a partition across brokers so a broker failure does not automatically lose the replicated data.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Replication when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, replicate a partition across brokers so a broker failure does not automatically lose the replicated data. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Replication as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Replication.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
