    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Sharding when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Sharding.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Sharding partitions data across independent shards so a single node does not have to hold all workload.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Split a large data set across shards using a key that spreads load and matches the dominant access pattern.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Sharding, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nSplit a large data set across shards using a key that spreads load and matches the dominant access pattern.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Sharding when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, split a large data set across shards using a key that spreads load and matches the dominant access pattern. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Sharding as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Sharding.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
