    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about CAP.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCAP describes a trade-off in distributed systems among consistency, availability, and partition tolerance during a network partition.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define CAP accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: CAP describes a trade-off in distributed systems among consistency, availability, and partition tolerance during a network partition.\n2. Use a concrete case: During a network partition, decide whether the affected operation favors consistency or availability based on the business requirement.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For CAP, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define CAP first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nDuring a network partition, decide whether the affected operation favors consistency or availability based on the business requirement.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use CAP when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, during a network partition, decide whether the affected operation favors consistency or availability based on the business requirement. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating CAP as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with CAP.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor CAP, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
