    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about CAP.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about CAP when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to CAP.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: CAP describes a trade-off in distributed systems among consistency, availability, and partition tolerance during a network partition.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: During a network partition, decide whether the affected operation favors consistency or availability based on the business requirement.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For CAP, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nDuring a network partition, decide whether the affected operation favors consistency or availability based on the business requirement.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use CAP when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, during a network partition, decide whether the affected operation favors consistency or availability based on the business requirement. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating CAP as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with CAP.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
