    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about Consistency.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nConsistency describes when reads observe writes according to the guarantees of a system.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Consistency accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Consistency describes when reads observe writes according to the guarantees of a system.\n2. Use a concrete case: After a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Consistency, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Consistency first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Consistency when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Consistency as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Consistency.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Consistency, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
