    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Consistency are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Consistency describes when reads observe writes according to the guarantees of a system.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Treating Consistency as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Consistency.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: After a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Consistency when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Consistency as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Consistency.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Consistency. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
