    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Consistency in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: After a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Consistency describes when reads observe writes according to the guarantees of a system.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: After a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case.\n4. Finish with the limitation: For Consistency, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Consistency when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Consistency as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Consistency.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Consistency in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: After a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Consistency describes when reads observe writes according to the guarantees of a system.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: After a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case.\n4. Finish with the limitation: For Consistency, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Consistency when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Consistency as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Consistency.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
