    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because availability describes whether a system continues to provide an acceptable response when requests arrive. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: use redundancy and a tested failover path so one component failure does not take down the entire service.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Availability addresses it: Availability describes whether a system continues to provide an acceptable response when requests arrive.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: Use redundancy and a tested failover path so one component failure does not take down the entire service.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: For Availability, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nUse redundancy and a tested failover path so one component failure does not take down the entire service.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Availability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use redundancy and a tested failover path so one component failure does not take down the entire service. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
