    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Availability comes from the behavior it provides: Availability describes whether a system continues to provide an acceptable response when requests arrive. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Use redundancy and a tested failover path so one component failure does not take down the entire service.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Availability describes whether a system continues to provide an acceptable response when requests arrive.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Use redundancy and a tested failover path so one component failure does not take down the entire service.\n3. State the trade-off: For Availability, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nUse redundancy and a tested failover path so one component failure does not take down the entire service.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Availability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use redundancy and a tested failover path so one component failure does not take down the entire service. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
