    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Availability are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Availability describes whether a system continues to provide an acceptable response when requests arrive.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: Use redundancy and a tested failover path so one component failure does not take down the entire service.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nUse redundancy and a tested failover path so one component failure does not take down the entire service.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Availability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use redundancy and a tested failover path so one component failure does not take down the entire service. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Availability. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
