    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about Availability.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Availability misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Availability. Availability describes whether a system continues to provide an acceptable response when requests arrive.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Availability: Availability describes whether a system continues to provide an acceptable response when requests arrive.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Use redundancy and a tested failover path so one component failure does not take down the entire service.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Availability, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nUse redundancy and a tested failover path so one component failure does not take down the entire service.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Availability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use redundancy and a tested failover path so one component failure does not take down the entire service. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
